\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Measurement error in causal inference.}
Foundations appear in \citet{carroll2006, gustafson2004, buonaccorsi2010, bound2001measurement}, with DAG-based formalization by \citet{hernan_cole2009} and \citet{kuroki_pearl2014}. For binary variables, non-differential misclassification biases toward the null~\cite{bross1954misclassification, brenner1993, ogburn_vanderweele2012}, but this fails for polytomous exposures~\cite{dosemeci1990, jurek2005, veirod_laake2001}. Extensions address differential measurement error~\cite{edwards_lessler_naimi2021, imai_yamamoto2010}, endogenous misreporting~\cite{nguimkeu_denteh_tchernis2019}, mismeasured covariates under IPW~\cite{shu_yi2019a, shu_yi2019b}, mediation with mismeasured mediators~\cite{valeri_vanderweele2013}, nonclassical error~\cite{schennach2020mismeasured}, and multiply robust estimation~\cite{wei2025multiply}. All analyze a single DAG topology; ours characterizes how the same confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across seven topologies.

\paragraph{Text classifiers and LLM annotation.}
\citet{grimmer2022} and \citet{feder_etal2022} provide foundations for text-as-data and causal inference in NLP. \citet{wood_doughty2018} first applied DAG reasoning to classifier error; \citet{teblunthuis2024} gave the most complete binary treatment ($K{=}2$, four DAGs); \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended to categorical LLM classifiers but covered only confounding. On annotation quality, \citet{gilardi2023chatgpt} and \citet{ziems2024can} document rapid LLM annotator adoption (surveyed by \citealt{tan2024llm}), \citet{baumann2025} found 31\% incorrect conclusions, and \citet{kim_garg2025} showed 60\% error correlation across models~\cite{pangakis_wolken_fasching2023, cheng_mayya_sedoc2024, variance_aware_llm2026, hidden_measurement_error2026}. \citet{audinet2025} benchmark debiasing; \citet{keith_jensen_oconnor2020} survey the landscape. These works either restrict to binary labels, cover a single topology, or diagnose quality without connecting it to causal validity.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis and correction methods.}
Our ASV framework draws on sensitivity analysis without assumptions~\cite{ding_vanderweele2016}, E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017, mathur2018}, robustness values~\cite{cinelli2020making}, and mediation sensitivity~\cite{imai_keele_yamamoto2010, pryzant_etal2021}, but parametrizes annotation error severity rather than omitted variable strength. \citet{lash2009} and \citet{blackwell_honaker_king2017} provide complementary frameworks; \citet{mahajan2006} and \citet{hu2008} establish identification and estimation under misclassified regressors, including nonlinear models with instrumental variables. For correction, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023} and DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} are topology-agnostic, with extensions by \citet{zrnic_candes2024, demirel2024prediction, cadei2025, gligoric2025}. We show this agnosticism is costly: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent, with matrix-based methods provably ineffective under linear FWL decomposition for four topologies.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt, ziems2024can, egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors (equivalently, misclassification) from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the structure of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference. Standard classification metrics do not capture this risk: within an $F_1$ range of 0.61--0.69 across three frontier LLMs, causal bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies.

The transition at $K{\geq}3$ is not gradual but structural. For $K{=}2$, the stochastic constraint forces any confusion matrix with positive diagonal to preserve coefficient signs---an algebraic property of the $2{\times}2$ matrix dimension~\cite{brenner1993}. This guarantee vanishes at $K{\geq}3$: the higher-dimensional confusion matrix admits negative effective weights on categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal even under high accuracy~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. Under a uniform Dirichlet prior, 83.4\% of $K{=}3$ exposure confusion matrices produce sign reversal of at least one secondary coefficient; under realistic accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), 2.1\% still do. The primary treatment coefficient is never reversed under the tested $\beta$ configurations where a single category dominates ($\beta_1 \gg \beta_2$)---an empirical regularity we discuss in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

Concretely, $F_1$ shows no rank correlation with causal bias ($\rho = -0.10$, $p = 0.45$ across 56 HumAID scenarios; bias ranges from 4.1\% to 42.1\% within $F_1$ 0.614--0.690). This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{dosemeci1990} and \citet{veirod_laake2001} noted the possibility of polytomous sign reversal; \citet{brenner1993} identified amplification for polytomous confounders; \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology. We present the first systematic analysis that jointly addresses multi-class ($K{\geq}3$) misclassification across multiple DAG topologies under structural equation models, extending prior binary or single-topology analyses~\cite{brenner1993, teblunthuis2024}. Our analytical predictions match Monte Carlo estimates within 1.6\% MAPE across 161 scenarios.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies\footnote{These span the four structural roles of the annotated variable: treatment (exposure), outcome-side covariate (confounding, mediation, M-bias, front-door), selection variable (collider), and instrument (IV).} (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification, establishing a sign-preservation/reversal dichotomy: five topologies preserve the treatment effect sign, while exposure and IV can reverse it (binarization amplifies this gap $>$150${\times}$, from $\bar{\Delta}{=}0.006$ for M-bias to $0.926$ for IV; Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}).

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values.} Building on the bias taxonomy, we define three ASV diagnostics---magnitude, significance, and sign-flip---with proved envelope monotonicity for $K{=}2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone}) and $K{=}3$ symmetric confusion matrices (Theorem~\ref{thm:k3_symmetric}), with $\varepsilon$-approximate monotonicity for general $K{\geq}3$ ($\varepsilon < 0.017$ across 545{,}945 adversarial curves spanning four structural families; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}). These reveal a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$), enabling topology-specific error tolerance assessment.

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction routing.} We observe that FWL invariance renders matrix-based corrections provably ineffective under linear regression for four covariate topologies, and evaluate five methods across 210 configurations---PPI++ harms M-bias ($-88\%$), MC-SIMEX degrades front-door ($-62.4\%$)---and provide a topology-aware decision procedure (Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction}, Appendix), validated end-to-end with production LLM confusion matrices from VAST stance detection (Appendix~\ref{sec:real_cm_demo}).
\end{enumerate}

Counterintuitively, applying corrections without topology awareness can worsen estimates: PPI++ harms M-bias by 88\% and topology-agnostic IV correction amplifies bias by 139\%.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We characterized how multi-class annotation errors interact with causal DAG topology: at $K{\geq}3$, exposure and IV can reverse treatment effect signs while five topologies preserve them. ASVs yield a topology-dependent vulnerability ranking; matrix-based corrections are provably ineffective under linear regression for four covariate topologies (FWL invariance). The practical implication is direct: identify the topology first---for four topologies, no matrix-based correction helps; for exposure, PPI++ with gold labels reduces bias by 93.6\%.

\section*{Limitations}

We assume non-differential misclassification (Appendix~\ref{sec:nondiff_stress}). Closed-form results (Theorems~1--2) assume linear SEMs with proportional effects ($f{=}\lambda g$); proportional effects is a reasonable approximation for ordinal classification (e.g., sentiment strength) where treatment shifts the distribution along an ordered scale, and sign preservation holds at 97.3\% under non-proportional effects (Table~\ref{tab:proportional_empirical}). FWL invariance extends to logistic models for covariate topologies (Appendix~\ref{app:fwl_proof}), but for exposure, logistic specification can increase bias (Appendix~\ref{sec:fwl_nonlinear}); practical recommendations do not directly extend to nonlinear models (matching, doubly-robust). The $C(\delta)$ linear interpolation assumes misclassification patterns are structurally stable as error severity varies; LLM quality changes across prompts may not follow this path. ASV computation requires estimating $B_{\mathrm{OV}}$ from gold labels via $C^{-1}$; error propagation under small gold-label budgets ($<$500) is an open question. The framework assumes a known DAG; DAG misspecification under measurement error may compound bias. General $K{\geq}3$ envelope monotonicity remains open; worst-case step deviation reaches 0.056 for exposure but empirically decreases with $K$ ($\varepsilon \propto K^{-\alpha}$, $\alpha \in [0.6, 1.5]$; Table~\ref{tab:conjecture_verify}), and Algorithm~1 routing is robust to violations below $\varepsilon{=}0.017$.

